  ],
  "text": "Your body runs its own clock, and it is not quite twenty four hours long. Shut people in a cave with no daylight and they drift. The clock runs slow. So it gets reset every single day, and the thing that resets it is light. Mostly blue light. A master clock in your brain reads that light and sets everything else by it. Temperature, alertness, hunger, hormones. All of it runs on that one timetable. Darkness triggers melatonin, which is not a sedative. It is a signal that night has started. So a bright screen at midnight is not just keeping you up. It is arguing about what time it is. Jet lag is the same argument. Your clock is still set to a place you have left. But the practical lever is the other end of the day. Morning daylight resets it harder. And for most people, going to bed at the same time beats going to bed for longer. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
